# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# GitHub Secrets & Variables — Expected Configuration
#
# SETUP:
#   cp github-secrets.example github-secrets         # single-account
#   cp github-secrets.example github-secrets.dev     # multi-account (per env)
#   cp github-secrets.example github-secrets.staging
#   cp github-secrets.example github-secrets.prod
#
# Fill in the values below, then run the VS Code task:
#   Single-account:  "AWS: Setup GitHub Repo Variables"
#   Multi-account:   "AWS: Setup GitHub Environment Variables" (once per env)
#
# The tasks push both GitHub variables AND the SENSITIVE_VARS secret in one go.
# All github-secrets* files are gitignored — they never leave your machine.
#
# FORMAT: KEY=VALUE (one per line, no quotes). Blank lines and lines starting
# with # are ignored.
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

# ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
# REPOSITORY-LEVEL VARIABLES (non-sensitive — set via VS Code task)
# These are shared across all environments.
# ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

# PROJECT_NAME=myproject
#   The project name prefix used for all AWS resources.
#   Set automatically by `AWS: Setup GitHub Repo Variables` from bootstrap outputs.

# ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
# PER-ENVIRONMENT VARIABLES (non-sensitive — set via VS Code task)
# Each environment (dev, staging, prod) has its own values.
# Set automatically by `AWS: Setup GitHub Environment Variables`.
# ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

# AWS_ACCOUNT_ID=123456789012
#   AWS account ID for the target environment.

# AWS_REGION=eu-west-2
#   AWS region for the target environment.

# ARTIFACT_BUCKET=myproject-dev-deploy-artifacts
#   S3 bucket name for Lambda ZIP deployment artifacts.

# ECR_REPO_URL=123456789012.dkr.ecr.eu-west-2.amazonaws.com/myproject-webapp
#   Full ECR repository URL for webapp Docker images.

# TFSTATE_BUCKET=myproject-dev-tfstate
#   S3 bucket name for Terraform remote state.

# TFSTATE_LOCK_TABLE=myproject-dev-tflock
#   DynamoDB table name for Terraform state locking.

# ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
# SENSITIVE VARS — injected into AWS Secrets Manager via Terraform
# ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
#
# These key=value pairs become the SENSITIVE_VARS GitHub secret — a JSON map
# that Terraform merges into the project-level Secrets Manager secret alongside
# Aurora DB URLs and Cognito IDs. Lambda services call SecretsConfig.resolve()
# at cold-start to read only the keys they need.
#
# HOW IT WORKS:
#   1. You fill in values below and run the VS Code task.
#   2. The task JSON-encodes the key=value pairs and pushes them as the
#      SENSITIVE_VARS GitHub secret (encrypted at rest by GitHub).
#   3. In the CD workflow, TF_VAR_sensitive_vars=${{ secrets.SENSITIVE_VARS }}
#      passes the JSON map to Terraform as a variable.
#   4. Terraform merges it into aws_secretsmanager_secret_version.project.
#   5. Lambda gets AWS_SECRETS_ARN as an env var and reads the secret JSON
#      at cold-start — no sensitive values ever appear as plain Lambda env vars.
#
# Add keys for any third-party service your Lambdas need at runtime.
# Leave values empty to exclude a key (empty values are ignored by the task).

# ── Example third-party API keys (fill in if used) ───────────────────────────

# FIRECRAWL_API_KEY=
#   FireCrawl web scraping API key (if used).

# SENTRY_DSN=
#   Sentry error tracking DSN (if used).

# STRIPE_SECRET_KEY=
#   Stripe payment API secret key (if used).

# OPENAI_API_KEY=
#   OpenAI API key (if used).

# ── Cognito seed admin user (required when seed-cognito deploy task is enabled) ──

# SEED_ADMIN_EMAIL=admin@yourcompany.com
#   Email address for the initial admin user created in Cognito during deployment.
#   ⚠ REQUIRED when the seed-cognito deploy task is active.
#   Cognito generates a random temporary password and emails it here.
#   The admin must change the password on first sign-in.

# SEED_ADMIN_USER_ID=
#   Domain user ID (UUID) stored as the admin's custom:userId Cognito attribute.
#   Used as the user's stable identifier in DynamoDB and JWTs.
#   Optional — a random UUID is auto-generated if not set, but an explicit value
#   ensures the ID is stable across re-deployments.
#   Generate one with: node -e "console.log(require('crypto').randomUUID())"

# ── Monitoring Dashboard — GitHub OAuth (optional) ───────────────────────────
#
# Required only if you deploy the monitoring dashboard and want GitHub OAuth.
# Create a GitHub OAuth App at https://github.com/settings/developers
#   → Homepage URL: your monitoring webapp Lambda URL
#   → Callback URL: your monitoring API Lambda URL + /api/auth/github/callback
#
# These 3 values become the MONITORING_OAUTH_VARS GitHub secret.
# MONITORING_JWT_SECRET, MONITORING_API_BASE_URL, and MONITORING_WEBAPP_URL
# are auto-managed by Terraform and the CD workflow — do NOT set them here.

# GITHUB_CLIENT_ID=
#   GitHub OAuth App Client ID.

# GITHUB_CLIENT_SECRET=
#   GitHub OAuth App Client Secret.

# GITHUB_REQUIRED_ORG=
#   GitHub organization slug. Only active members of this org can sign in.

# ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
# VALUES that are NOT GitHub secrets (common misconceptions)
# ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
#
# AWS_ACCESS_KEY_ID / AWS_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY
#   ❌ NEVER store these. All AWS auth uses GitHub OIDC → IAM role assumption.
#   The bootstrap module creates the OIDC provider and deploy role.
#   Workflows use `aws-actions/configure-aws-credentials@v4` with `role-to-assume`.
#
# Cognito User Pool ID / Client ID
#   ❌ Not secrets — non-sensitive identifiers. Terraform injects them as
#   Lambda env vars automatically via resolved_infra_vars.
#
# Database URLs (ORDERS_DATABASE_URL, etc.)
#   ❌ Not GitHub secrets — they live in AWS Secrets Manager.
#   Terraform creates the secret. Lambda reads it at cold-start via
#   `SecretsConfig.resolve()` from the `AWS_SECRETS_ARN` env var.
#
# S3 Bucket Names / SQS Queue URLs / DynamoDB Table Names
#   ❌ These are injected by Terraform via the service registry `envVars` array.
#   No manual configuration needed.
